  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Resilient, Innovative and Sustainable Debt Management</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The core delivery mechanism involves close technical collaboration with development partners, namely, IMF, WAIFEM, MEFMI, CARTAC, SARTTAC, and the World Bank Group, for scoping debt vulnerabilities in consultation with countries and developing tailored approaches to support member countries&apos; capacity. The approach is to focus on short-term capacity supplementation (in case of urgency) and long-term capacity building (with a programmtic approach). This ensures that the in-country technical assistance, knowledge sharing, training, and capacity building provided are aligned with global best practices and immediate macroeconomic realities. Furthermore, delivery is significantly scaled through strategic partnerships with major regional and international institutions. Leveraging these development partners ensures technical assistance, training workshops are nationally and regionally relevant, cost-effective, and provide broad coverage across member countries, maximising the reach and impact of improved public debt management practices. The Commonwealth Secretariat&apos;s Debt Management Programme offers distinct advantages to its member states, rooted in its unique alignment with their needs and institutional context. Its core differentiator is the Home-Grown Commonwealth Meridian System, a tailored, accessible tool specifically designed for efficient public debt recording and analysis in member countries, regardless of their institutional capacity. The Programme maintains a deep and specialised focus on vulnerable economies, including SIDS, Landlocked, and Least Developed Countries, offering nuanced strategies to manage the unique constraints and climate-related fiscal vulnerabilities of these nations. Furthermore, the Secretariat&apos;s approach is characterised by non-political neutrality and longstanding trust established over four decades of engagement with Ministries of Finance and Central Banks, fostering an environment where nations can implement objective debt solutions free from geopolitical or development partner pressures. Finally, the provision of cost-effective and accessible solutions ensures that high-quality technical support and policy guidance are available even to lower-income members without imposing excessive financial burdens.</narrative>
  </description>
